{
  "concept": "A complex number x + yi can be located either by its rectangular coordinates (x, y) or by its polar pair (r, θ), and the right triangle in the complex plane converts between them: x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ, r = √(x²+y²).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal double-headed thin arrow through the origin labeled 'Real' at its right end",
    "Vertical double-headed thin arrow through the origin labeled 'Imaginary' at its top",
    "Origin point at the intersection of the two axes",
    "Filled dark dot in the first quadrant representing the complex number",
    "Label 'x + yi' in dark blue placed just above-right of the dot",
    "Straight dark-blue segment from the origin to the dot (the hypotenuse), labeled 'r' at its midpoint, offset to the upper-left",
    "Vertical dark-blue segment dropping from the dot to the real axis, labeled 'y' to its right",
    "Horizontal dark-blue segment from the origin to the foot of the vertical drop, labeled 'x' below it",
    "Small right-angle square marker at the foot of the vertical drop",
    "Small arc at the origin between the positive real axis and the hypotenuse, labeled 'θ'",
    "Heavy dark-blue circular arc of radius r centered at the origin, passing through the dot, with arrowheads at both ends (one sweeping left past the imaginary axis, one sweeping down past the real axis)",
    "Optional readout panel showing current values of x, y, r, θ and the two forms 'x + yi' and 'r(cos θ + i sin θ)'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "theta",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Direction angle θ (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 360, 1],
      "default": 55,
      "teaches": "Rotating the point along a fixed circle changes x and y together while r stays constant — θ controls direction only, and the legs shrink/grow and change sign as the point crosses quadrants."
    },
    {
      "id": "modulus",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Modulus r",
      "range": [0.5, 5, 0.1],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "r is literally the distance from the origin to the point: increasing it scales the circle, the hypotenuse, and both legs proportionally without changing the direction."
    },
    {
      "id": "labelMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Annotation style",
      "options": ["Rectangular (x, y)", "Polar (r, θ)", "Both"],
      "default": "Both",
      "teaches": "The same fixed point carries two equivalent descriptions; swapping the labels shows that x + yi and r(cos θ + i sin θ) name one location, not two."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The point and its triangle",
      "narration": "A complex number x + yi is plotted as a point in the plane, with the real part measured along the horizontal axis and the imaginary part along the vertical axis. Dropping a perpendicular to the real axis forms a right triangle whose legs are exactly x and y.",
      "state": { "theta": 55, "modulus": 3, "labelMode": "Rectangular (x, y)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Naming the hypotenuse and the angle",
      "narration": "The hypotenuse from the origin is the modulus r, and the angle it makes with the positive real axis is θ. These two numbers locate the point just as completely as x and y do.",
      "state": { "theta": 55, "modulus": 3, "labelMode": "Polar (r, θ)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep θ around the circle",
      "narration": "Drag θ and the point rides the heavy circular arc: r never changes, but x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ stretch, shrink, and change sign as the point moves through each quadrant.",
      "state": { "theta": 145, "modulus": 3, "labelMode": "Both" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change r, keep the direction",
      "narration": "Now hold θ fixed and grow r. The circle and the hypotenuse expand together and both legs scale by the same factor — r really is distance from the origin, not an extra label.",
      "state": { "theta": 145, "modulus": 4.5, "labelMode": "Both" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two names, one point",
      "narration": "Switch the annotations back and forth: the dot never moves, yet it reads as x + yi in rectangular form and r(cos θ + i sin θ) in polar form. The triangle is the dictionary between them.",
      "state": { "theta": 55, "modulus": 3, "labelMode": "Both" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "r and θ are not new information — they are the hypotenuse and base angle of the very triangle whose legs are x and y, so trigonometry translates directly between x + yi and r(cos θ + i sin θ).",
  "keyInsight_note": "",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White background, single panel. Thin black double-headed arrows form the axes through a centered origin (origin sits slightly left-of-center and low, so the first quadrant dominates). Axis labels 'Real' (right, italic bold) and 'Imaginary' (top, italic bold). All construction lines — hypotenuse, vertical leg, horizontal leg, and the heavy circular arc — in a dark navy blue; the arc is noticeably thicker than the triangle edges and carries arrowheads at both ends so it reads as a full circle being traced. The point is a solid black/navy dot with the label 'x + yi' in navy just above-right. Labels 'r' sits along the hypotenuse offset up-left, 'y' to the right of the vertical leg, 'x' below the horizontal leg, and 'θ' just inside a small arc at the origin. Include a faint right-angle marker where the vertical leg meets the real axis. When sliders move, the dot, triangle legs, angle arc, and circle radius all update live; a small text readout near a corner shows x, y, r, θ and both algebraic forms. Labels fade in/out according to the toggle rather than the geometry changing."
}

