      "state": {
        "theta": 145,
        "modulus": 4.5,
        "labelMode": "Both"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two names, one point",
      "narration": "Switch the annotations back and forth: the dot never moves, yet it reads as x + yi in rectangular form and r(cos θ + i sin θ) in polar form. The triangle is the dictionary between them.",
      "state": {
        "theta": 55,
        "modulus": 3,
        "labelMode": "Both"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "r and θ are not new information — they are the hypotenuse and base angle of the very triangle whose legs are x and y, so trigonometry translates directly between x + yi and r(cos θ + i sin θ).",
  "keyInsight_note": "",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White background, single panel. Thin black double-headed arrows form the axes through a centered origin (origin sits slightly left-of-center and low, so the first quadrant dominates). Axis labels 'Real' (right, italic bold) and 'Imaginary' (top, italic bold). All construction lines — hypotenuse, vertical leg, horizontal leg, and the heavy circular arc — in a dark navy blue; the arc is noticeably thicker than the triangle edges and carries arrowheads at both ends so it reads as a full circle being traced. The point is a solid black/navy dot with the label 'x + yi' in navy just above-right. Labels 'r' sits along the hypotenuse offset up-left, 'y' to the right of the vertical leg, 'x' below the horizontal leg, and 'θ' just inside a small arc at the origin. Include a faint right-angle marker where the vertical leg meets the real axis. When sliders move, the dot, triangle legs, angle arc, and circle radius all update live; a small text readout near a corner shows x, y, r, θ and both algebraic forms. Labels fade in/out according to the toggle rather than the geometry changing."
}

